WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.880
After an excruciatingly long wait, Eddie Brock&nbsp;
and Venom have finally returned to theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.560
so let's dig in and talk about the&nbsp;
ending of "Venom: Let There Be Carnage."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.720
Obviously, many spoilers lie ahead.&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.240
"There's gonna be carnage."

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.760
Cletus Cassidy believes that people&nbsp;
love a serial killer — Just ask him.&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.680
"People love serial killers."
 
See?&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.160
But by the end of the film, Cletus has had Carnage&nbsp;
run roughshod all over him, lost the only woman he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.560
has ever loved, and finally revealed his true&nbsp;
motive: he just wanted to be Eddie's friend.&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.520
"Miss you. So much."
As Venom is quick to point out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.680
that position is already filled. Which is&nbsp;
most likely why, after nearly two hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.280
doing battle with him, Venom chomps off Cletus'&nbsp;
head. Sorry, Cletus, it was fun while it lasted.&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.680
As for the issue of Carnage, a crawling ball&nbsp;
of goo searching desperately for a new host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.560
Venom takes care of that problem too. Scooping up&nbsp;
the red sludge, Venom sucks it up like a Slurpee.&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.240
In the comics, of course, Carnage is like&nbsp;
a bad penny who just keeps turning up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.120
But in the "Venom" franchise, at&nbsp;
least for the foreseeable future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.240
Cletus and Carnage both seem like&nbsp;
they've taken that long, last trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.080
to the distant cathedral.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.160
Fans were thrilled to learn that Frances Barrison&nbsp;
would be a key character in "Venom: Let There Be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.400
Carnage," and even more thrilled to learn that&nbsp;
she'd be brought to life by Naomie Harris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.880
Harris is a veteran actor who has shined in&nbsp;
films as varied as "Skyfall" and "Moonlight."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.560
Fans will undoubtedly be happy with the&nbsp;
result, as the character more commonly known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.800
as Shriek features prominently throughout&nbsp;
the film as the Bonnie to Kasady's Clyde,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.960
cackling with glee as the ill-fated lovers&nbsp;
go on a super-powered killing spree.&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.640
By the end of the movie, however, the love&nbsp;
triangle of Cletus, Frances, and Carnage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.280
is beginning to show some strain. For starters,&nbsp;
Shriek's main weapon is a brain-scrambling scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.000
that doesn't pair well with symbiotes' sensitivity&nbsp;
to high sonic energy. Every time Carnage seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.800
to be getting into a groove, Shriek lets out a&nbsp;
scream, knocking the big red guy off his game.&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.600
After she seemingly dispatches Detective Mulligan,&nbsp;
Shriek has one final confrontation with the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.640
symbiotes. Echoing early comic book appearances&nbsp;
of Venom, this smackdown involves a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.280
church bell whose every ring pummels the senses&nbsp;
of the symbiotes. Knocked off her perch during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.920
battle, Shriek falls to her seeming death.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.520
A great deal of "Let There Be Carnage"&nbsp;
concerns itself with the uneasy, highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.320
non-traditional relationship between Eddie and&nbsp;
Venom. Sharing a goal, an apartment, and a body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.640
the duo undoubtedly get on each other's nerves,&nbsp;
but need each other to survive and thrive.&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.640
By the end of the film, this odd couple seems&nbsp;
to have finally made up. The immediate villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.160
are gone, and they've turned their focus on&nbsp;
the next adventure — one that will seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.400
interact with "Spider-Man: No Way Home." But&nbsp;
first, Venom needs some well-deserved R&amp;R.&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.640
Perhaps not since the training scene of&nbsp;
Rocky III has it felt so good to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.120
two heroes frolicking together on a beach.
Most importantly, Venom has indeed come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.120
of the so-called "Eddie Brock closet" for&nbsp;
good. The journalist seems more comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.000
with letting his freak flag fly, as long as Venom&nbsp;
does its best to only feast on those who really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.520
really deserve it.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.280
A key character in this film is Detective&nbsp;
Mulligan, played by the always excellent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.600
always intense character actor Stephen Graham.&nbsp;
In the opening scenes of "Carnage," we see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.120
young Mulligan attempting to thwart an&nbsp;
escape by Shriek, shooting her in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.160
eye but nearly losing his life in the process.
Clearly, Frances Barrison knows how to hold a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.960
grudge as decades later, Shriek pins the detective&nbsp;
to the ground and presses a thumb into his eye.&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.240
After the perceived death of Shriek,&nbsp;
we see a very brief shot that not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.480
only indicates Graham is still alive, but&nbsp;
that he might also now be super-powered.&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.400
What does this mean? Who could Mulligan&nbsp;
become? Fans will undoubtedly spend the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.280
immediate aftermath of the film's release poring&nbsp;
over possibilities, but perhaps we could start the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.720
conversation by throwing out one: Toxin.
In the 2004 Venom/Carnage miniseries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.600
Carnage created Toxin, who then bonded&nbsp;
with an NYPD cop named Patrick Mulligan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.720
Mulligan ended up talking things&nbsp;
out with Spider-Man and agreeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.200
to try and harness the symbiote, who could be&nbsp;
considered Venom's grandson, to fight crime.&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.920
It's also well known that in the comics, Shriek&nbsp;
can use her powers to simply turn people evil, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.120
perhaps Mulligan is merely now an evil Mulligan.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.200
Perhaps the most significant revelation in the&nbsp;
final moments of "Let There Be Carnage" comes at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.120
the end. This mid-credit sequence depicts&nbsp;
Venom and Eddie in a hotel room together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.800
watching foreign language soap operas.
As the two begin exchanging thoughts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:08.320
the show they're watching, Venom makes an&nbsp;
intoxicating offer — what if he were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.560
give Eddie Brock just the tiniest glimpse of&nbsp;
all that's going through his superior brain?&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.680
Eddie opts in. Once Venom begins the download, the&nbsp;
room starts spinning, lights start flashing — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.640
Eddie wakes up in a very different room, this one&nbsp;
playing a different program on the television.&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.040
Sure enough, it's J. Jonah Jameson, making his&nbsp;
multiverse-shattering announcement that Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:32.400
is really Peter Parker. Venom gets in close to the&nbsp;
TV to take in the news, and when he sees Parker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.160
he begins to lick the television.
"I want Spider-Maaaaaan!"&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:42.000
As fans have seen in the "No Way Home" trailer,&nbsp;
Jameson's announcement is the catalyst for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.280
everything that happens in that film&nbsp;
— causing Peter to seek out Doctor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.560
Strange to presumably meddle with the&nbsp;
multiverse in a way that allows such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.200
previous franchise threats as Doctor Octopus&nbsp;
and Electro to once again pose a threat.&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.720
Of course, the Venom we see in these&nbsp;
Tom Hardy films is a lot nicer than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.560
character is typically portrayed — but even&nbsp;
if he is putting the "hero" in anti-hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.520
it seems likely that Venom will not be approaching&nbsp;
Spider-Man with a tentacle extended in friendship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.080
At the very least, this mid-credits sequence&nbsp;
establishes that this film's conclusion coincides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.480
with the "Far From Home" opening — which&nbsp;
makes a crossover feel quite inevitable.&nbsp;

